**Examples:** [six programs](02_more/01_examples/index.html), from a cube in raw OpenGL to marching cubes with ImGui,
each with another interface and the trap it teaches. **Reference:** [the bridge](02_more/02_reference/index.html)
between the program and the slide (two C++ files, one HTML page, one script).

## What you need

- A slide tool that can show a web page: HTML slides ([reveal.js](https://revealjs.com/),
  [Slidev](https://sli.dev/), [Quarto](https://quarto.org/), [lhtml-site](https://scholartools.fr/lhtml/)...),
  or another tool with the workarounds of [step 2](01_tutorial/02_in-your-slides/index.html).
- For your own program: C++ with OpenGL 3.3 (with GLFW, or a library such as
  [CGP](https://github.com/drohmer/cgp-2027)), and the [Emscripten SDK](https://emscripten.org/).
- For a model file only: nothing to compile.

The code of the tutorial, its examples and their tests: [interactive-3d-slides]({{ repo }}).
